 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nLambda default argument resolution broken\n\n#### Description\n\nLambda functions with default arguments are not resolving their default values correctly. When a lambda has a default parameter and is called without providing that parameter, the inference fails to properly resolve the default value.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport astroid\n\ncode = '''\nmirror = lambda x=None: x\n\nun = mirror(1)\n'''\n\nast = astroid.parse(code)\ninferred = list(ast.igetattr('un'))\nprint(f\"Inferred type: {type(inferred[0])}\")\nprint(f\"Inferred value: {inferred[0].value}\")\n```\n\nExpected: The inferred value should be `1` (the argument passed to the lambda)\nActual: The inference fails or returns incorrect results\n\nThis also affects more complex lambda scenarios with default arguments:\n\n```python\nimport astroid\n\ncode = '''\nfunc = lambda a, b=42, *args: args\nresult = func(1, 2, 3)\n'''\n\nast = astroid.parse(code)\n# This should infer a tuple (3,) but fails\n```\n\nThe issue appears to be related to how lambda functions handle scope resolution when default arguments are present.\n</issue>\n",
